Enterprise RCA Multi-Agent System — Copilot Build Plan

1. Mission

Act as a Senior AI Agent Architect and Senior Enterprise Software Engineer.

Design and build a production-grade, multi-agent Root Cause Analysis (RCA) investigation system using native GitHub Copilot Agent capabilities inside Visual Studio Code.

The objective is to transform the existing single-agent RCA investigation process into a coordinated multi-agent investigation system.

The system must:

- Accept an incident ID, onboarding ID, API name, error message, process instance ID, or failure description.
- Automatically plan the investigation.
- Discover the relevant source code using CUO Graph.
- Collect runtime, log, trace, database, workflow and deployment evidence.
- Delegate independent investigations to specialized subagents.
- Execute independent investigations concurrently where supported.
- Correlate all findings.
- Validate the actual root cause against source code and runtime evidence.
- Generate a comprehensive, technically accurate RCA.

Do not build another chatbot. Build an investigation engine using Copilot's agent orchestration capabilities.

2. Critical constraints

Before implementation, understand and respect these requirements.

1. Do NOT modify, overwrite, rename or delete the existing "rca.instructions.md".
2. Treat the existing RCA instructions as the authoritative investigation policy.
3. Do NOT replace existing CLI tools with MCP servers.
4. Do NOT introduce Google ADK, LangChain, CrewAI or another external agent framework.
5. Reuse existing CLI tools and their installed skills.
6. Do not invent CLI commands, arguments, tool names or output schemas.
7. Inspect the installed Copilot capabilities and actual tool availability before designing agents.
8. Do not assume that creating custom agents automatically guarantees parallel execution.
9. Preserve environment-specific investigation rules, especially Azure-specific routing.
10. Keep all investigation operations read-only unless the user explicitly authorizes a change.
11. Do not expose credentials, access tokens or customer-sensitive data.
12. Do not claim an investigation was completed if a required source was not queried.

